theory RHTNLocalExchange
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN local device-to-device exchange -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `local/`, a third tree, and it needed to be one.
`wire-only/` holds properties a third party checks from bytes it receives;
there is no third party here and no record is produced.  `compliant/` holds
obligations only the acting party can check; this is not an obligation but an
exchange under an adversary.  What `local/` holds is a **two-party exchange
across two channels of different strength** (`wire-format.md` §14.3), and the
question is which of its guarantees come from the bytes and which come from
the physical premise.

WHAT IS MODELLED.  §14.3's exchange, to the point where a bearer-carried
message is accepted:

  1. each device shows its 16-byte contribution optically;
  2. both derive the ceremony-id, SHA-256 over the tag and the two
     contributions (design §7.5.2), and each shows it;
  3. each checks the other's ceremony-id against its own -- §14.3.1's
     "a mismatch is where a man in the middle shows";
  4. a bearer carries an anchored message, whose anchor and echoed
     contribution the receiver checks against what it read optically
     (§14.3.2).

  ONE RULE PAIR STANDS FOR ALL FIVE ANCHORED MESSAGES.  `IntentExchange`,
  `BundleContinuation`, `ProximityOutcomes`, `CandidateHandover` and
  `CaptureKeyHandover` differ in payload and share their entire checking discipline -- anchor against own
  ceremony-id, and for the intent the echoed contribution as well.  The
  payload is opaque here, so the result covers all five.  `DeviceIntroduction` and `DeviceCredential`
  are NOT modelled: §14.3.3 states outright that they carry no anchor, so
  there is nothing for a symbolic model to check that the section does not
  already concede.

THE TWO CHANNELS, and this is the whole of the theory's content.

  OPTICAL: **authentic, and confidential to whoever looks.**  A `!Optical`
  fact is created only by the rules below, so the adversary cannot inject one
  -- that is §14.1's non-intermediability, taken as the premise it is.  The
  adversary learns an optical value only by firing
  `Adversary_Reads_A_Screen`, which records an `Observed` action.

  **This changed on 2026-10-05.**  Until then every optical rule also wrote
  its value to `Out`, because §14.3.1 said neither value was secret.  The
  author's ruling makes the two contributions key the local session, so they
  are secret from anyone who did not see a screen, and reading a screen
  became an act the trace records rather than a freebie.

  BEARER: **fully adversarial as a transport, and carrying ciphertext.**
  `Out` and `In`, nothing else; Bluetooth, a local radio, or a fetch through
  FuerosNet, since §14.3 leaves the choice to the shell and ranks it by
  locality rather than integrity.  What crosses it is now one `senc` under a
  key derived from the two contributions, so the adversary's position on the
  transport is as strong as ever and what it can do there depends on whether
  it looked at the screens.

WHAT IS ESTABLISHED, AND WHAT IS NOT.  Two positive properties now.
`an_accepted_anchor_was_derived_by_both`: a message accepted over the bearer
carries a ceremony-id both devices independently computed and each read off
the other's screen.  It is mutation-tested -- delete the anchor comparison and
it falsifies.

Two more, added 2026-10-05, which are the two halves of what the session
encryption buys and are stated as what they are -- properties conditioned on
an action, not claims that the adversary is weaker.
`a_payload_is_secret_unless_a_screen_was_read`: nobody who did not look can
READ a carriage, which is what the prekey share rests on.
`a_payload_accepted_with_no_screen_read_was_sent`: nobody who did not look
can INJECT one.  **Exclusivity of the local channel is these two together**,
derived from the key rather than assumed; the bearer itself stays `Out` and
`In`, as permissive as it ever was.

  **The anchor still does not authenticate the sender, and still does not
  prevent replay into another ceremony.**  Both remain `exists-trace` lemmas
  that VERIFY, because the attack remains reachable -- to an adversary that
  read the screens.  The anchor was never what authenticated anything, and
  the session key does not change that; it changes who can reach the anchor
  at all.  What the anchor check buys is unchanged: a payload cannot be
  accepted under an anchor nobody derived.

  WHY THAT IS NOT A DEFECT IN THE DESIGN, and how much of it the model can
  now say.  §14.3.1 puts the man-in-the-middle resistance in one sentence --
  "each party reads both values off a screen it is looking at" -- and the cost
  of reading those screens is physical presence, which is the cost design §1
  meters.  **Dolev-Yao still has no locality**, and this theory does not
  invent any: the adversary may read every screen it likes.  What the
  `Observed` action adds is the ability to ASK whether it did, which is enough
  to separate the adversary standing over the two phones from the one with a
  radio in the next room.  The boundary is therefore drawn in the same place
  and is now visible from inside the model: past it the guarantee is
  physical, and §14.1 states that as a goal nothing can verify.

LIMITS, stated as the ceremony theories state theirs.
  - **The contribution ordering is a projection.**  design §7.5.2 orders the
    two contributions by ascending participant keyhash; the model fixes the
    initiator's first.  That is what the ordering rule achieves -- one
    canonical term both sides compute -- and an implementation getting the
    order wrong produces two different ceremony-ids, which is the mismatch
    step 3 catches and not a property this theory adds.
  - **The payload is opaque.**  Nothing here models what an intent's bundle
    is worth: a bundle entry's integrity comes from its own signatures
    (`wire-format.md` §5.3 requires each to verify alone), not from the
    anchor, so bearer substitution cannot manufacture a qualifying record it
    does not already hold.  That is the wire theories' subject, not this one's.
  - **Confidentiality is modelled as the ruling defines it and no further.**
    One `senc` over the whole anchored message, under one key, with no object
    carrying an encryption of its own [author, 2026-10-05: *"do not be
    ambiguous in such a way that this could be interpreted as a distinct,
    nested encryption"*].  The key is symbolic: `senc` is perfect here, so
    this says nothing about a cipher, a nonce discipline or key reuse across
    carriages.  §14.3.2 now fixes all three -- AES-256-GCM, a random 96-bit
    nonce per carriage in front of the ciphertext, one sealing over the whole
    object -- and none of them is a symbolic property, so the theory neither
    checks them nor claims to.  Matching `senc(..)` in `Bearer_Take`'s premise models
    an **AEAD**: a term that is not a well-formed plaintext under the key does
    not match, which is what an authenticated mode gives and what the wire
    format must therefore specify.  A bare stream cipher would not earn this
    rule.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: hashing, symmetric-encryption

restriction Distinct:
  "All a b #i. Distinct(a, b) @ #i ==> not(a = b)"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE OPTICAL EXCHANGE -- authentic, and public
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Each rule mints one contribution and shows it.  The `!Optical` fact is the
  screen: unforgeable, because no adversary rule produces one.  The `Out` is
  the same value in the clear, because a QR held up between two phones hides
  nothing from a third camera.
*/

rule Initiator_Shows_Contribution:
    [ Fr(~ca) ]
  --[ Showed($A, $B, ~ca), Distinct($A, $B) ]->
    [ !Optical($A, $B, 'contribution', ~ca)
    , Init1($A, $B, ~ca) ]

rule Responder_Shows_Contribution:
    [ Fr(~cb) ]
  --[ Showed($B, $A, ~cb), Distinct($B, $A) ]->
    [ !Optical($B, $A, 'contribution', ~cb)
    , Resp1($B, $A, ~cb) ]

/*
  THE ONE WAY THE ADVERSARY LEARNS AN OPTICAL VALUE, and the whole of what
  this theory gained when the carriages became confidential.

  Before the 2026-10-05 ruling every optical rule also wrote its value to
  `Out`, because §14.3.1 said neither value was secret.  Now a contribution
  keys the local session, so the value is secret from anyone who did not see
  the screen -- and seeing a screen is an ACT, recorded as `Observed`.

  **This is how locality enters a Dolev-Yao model.**  The adversary may still
  read everything, exactly as before, by firing this rule; what changed is
  that the trace then says so, and a lemma can ask whether it did.  Nothing
  here claims the adversary is weaker.  It makes the model able to
  distinguish the adversary who stood over the two phones from the one with a
  radio in the next room, which is the only distinction the ruling buys and
  the distinction §14.3.1's physical premise has always rested on.
*/

rule Adversary_Reads_A_Screen:
    [ !Optical($A, $B, kind, value) ]
  --[ Observed($A, $B, kind, value) ]->
    [ Out(value) ]

/*
  THE CEREMONY-ID, derived and then shown (design §7.5.2, §14.3.1).  Both
  rules build the identical term -- the initiator's contribution first -- for
  the reason the document orders by keyhash: the two devices must compute one
  value, not two.
*/

rule Initiator_Confirms:
    [ Init1($A, $B, ca), !Optical($B, $A, 'contribution', cb) ]
  --[ Derived($A, $B, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]->
    [ !Optical($A, $B, 'confirm', h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>))
    , Init2($A, $B, ca, cb, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]

rule Responder_Confirms:
    [ Resp1($B, $A, cb), !Optical($A, $B, 'contribution', ca) ]
  --[ Derived($B, $A, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]->
    [ !Optical($B, $A, 'confirm', h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>))
    , Resp2($B, $A, cb, ca, h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony', ca, cb>)) ]

/*
  THE CHECK THAT ANCHORS.  Each device reads the other's ceremony-id and
  compares it with its own; a mismatch stops the ceremony (§14.3.1), which
  here is the `Eq` restriction refusing the trace.
*/

rule Initiator_Anchors:
    [ Init2($A, $B, ca, cb, cid), !Optical($B, $A, 'confirm', theirs) ]
  --[ Eq(theirs, cid), Anchored($A, $B, cid) ]->
    [ AnchorA($A, $B, ca, cb, cid) ]

rule Responder_Anchors:
    [ Resp2($B, $A, cb, ca, cid), !Optical($A, $B, 'confirm', theirs) ]
  --[ Eq(theirs, cid), Anchored($B, $A, cid) ]->
    [ AnchorB($B, $A, cb, ca, cid) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE BEARER -- everything the adversary holds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  One send and one take, standing for all five anchored messages.  The
  payload is fresh on the honest path and any term the adversary likes on the
  other, which is exactly the substitution §14.3.2's echo check is there to
  catch.

  **ONE ENCRYPTION, AT THE SESSION LAYER** [author, 2026-10-05]: *"do not be
  ambiguous in such a way that this could be interpreted as a distinct,
  nested encryption."*  The whole anchored message is one `senc` under one
  key.  The objects inside are unchanged -- the anchor is still compared, the
  echo still checked -- and no object carries an encryption of its own.  The
  `CaptureKeyHandover` is protected here because it rides this, not because
  anything wraps it twice.

  THE KEY is `h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony-session', ca, cb>)`: the two contributions
  under a label distinct from the ceremony-id's `'rhtn/1:ceremony'`, so one
  construction does not both key a secret and name a public value.  The
  adversary derives it exactly when it has both contributions, which is to
  say exactly when it read both screens.
*/

rule Bearer_Send:
    [ AnchorA($A, $B, ca, cb, cid), Fr(~payload) ]
  --[ Sent($A, $B, cid, ~payload) ]->
    [ Out(senc(<'anchored', cid, ca, ~payload>,
               h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony-session', ca, cb>))) ]

rule Bearer_Take:
    [ AnchorB($B, $A, cb, ca, cid)
    , In(senc(<'anchored', anchor, echo, payload>,
              h(<'rhtn/1:ceremony-session', ca, cb>))) ]
  --[ Eq(anchor, cid)                 // the anchor, against this device's own
    , Eq(echo, ca)                    // the echoed contribution (§14.3.2)
    // THE ANCHOR AS THE MESSAGE CARRIED IT, not as this device computed it.
    // Recording the device's own `cid` here would make the lemma below hold
    // through `Responder_Anchors`' optical confirm whatever the bearer said,
    // and deleting the comparison above would leave it verifying -- a green
    // lemma for an unchecked field, which is the failure the theory
    // mutations exist to catch.
    , Accepted($B, $A, anchor, payload) ]->
    [ ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY, and it must exclude the adversary doing the work: an honest run in
// which the payload accepted is the payload sent.
lemma exchange_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex A B cid p #s #i.
       Sent(A, B, cid, p) @ #s
     & Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     & #s < #i"

/*  THE ONE POSITIVE PROPERTY.  A message accepted over the bearer carries a
    ceremony-id that BOTH devices independently derived -- so the anchor
    cannot be a value the adversary chose, and cannot be one only a single
    party ever computed.  That is ceremony-binding, and it is all of it.

    MUTATION-TESTED: replace `Eq(anchor, cid)` in `Bearer_Take` with a
    tautology and this falsifies, the adversary then supplying an anchor
    nobody derived.                                                         */
lemma an_accepted_anchor_was_derived_by_both:
  "All B A cid p #i.
     Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     ==> (Ex #x. Derived(A, B, cid) @ #x & #x < #i)
       & (Ex #y. Derived(B, A, cid) @ #y & #y < #i)"

/*  THE FIRST BOUNDARY, and it still VERIFIES because the attack is still
    reachable -- but only now to an adversary that FIRED
    `Adversary_Reads_A_Screen`.  Before the 2026-10-05 ruling it was reachable
    for free.  The echo catches a bearer contradicting the screen, never one
    quoting it, and quoting it now costs standing where the screens are.    */
lemma the_anchor_does_not_authenticate_the_sender:
  exists-trace
  "Ex B A cid p #i.
       Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     & not(Ex #s. Sent(A, B, cid, p) @ #s)"

/*  THE SECOND BOUNDARY, same cause and the same change: re-anchoring needs
    the second ceremony's id, echo AND session key, so it needs that
    ceremony's screens.  Ceremony-binding holds against a party who did not
    watch and not against one who did, which is what it always said; the
    cost of watching is now the cost of reading two screens rather than
    nothing.                                                                 */
lemma a_payload_is_re_anchorable_into_another_ceremony:
  exists-trace
  "Ex A B cid1 cid2 p #s #i.
       Sent(A, B, cid1, p) @ #s
     & Accepted(B, A, cid2, p) @ #i
     & not(cid1 = cid2)"

/*  WHAT THE 2026-10-05 RULING BUYS, and the reason the theory was touched at
    all.  A contribution is secret unless a screen was read: the only rule
    that writes one to `Out` is `Adversary_Reads_A_Screen`, and firing it is
    recorded.  Carried as `reuse` because the two lemmas below rest on it.

    MUTATION-TESTED 2026-10-05: restoring `Out` to EITHER contribution rule
    falsifies this in 4 steps, which is what it should do -- that
    contribution is then public and no screen was read to make it so.                                                                      */
lemma a_contribution_is_secret_unless_a_screen_was_read [reuse]:
  "All A B c #s #k.
       Showed(A, B, c) @ #s
     & K(c) @ #k
     ==> (Ex X Y kind #o. Observed(X, Y, kind, c) @ #o)"

/*  THE CONFIDENTIALITY HALF, which is what the prekey share rests on
    [author, 2026-10-05]: the share is *"protected in flight by the classical
    encryption of this local direct channel and visible only to the
    participants who need it."*  A payload sent over the bearer is secret
    unless a screen was read.  Without this lemma the theory would establish
    that nobody else can INJECT into the channel while saying nothing about
    whether anybody else can READ it, and reading is the direction a prekey
    cares about.

    MUTATION-TESTED 2026-10-05: restoring `Out` to BOTH contribution rules
    falsifies this in 7 steps, the adversary then deriving the session key
    and decrypting every carriage.  Restoring it to ONE leaves it verified;
    the note under the integrity lemma says why that matters.                              */
lemma a_payload_is_secret_unless_a_screen_was_read:
  "All A B cid p #s #k.
       Sent(A, B, cid, p) @ #s
     & K(p) @ #k
     ==> (Ex X Y kind v #o. Observed(X, Y, kind, v) @ #o)"

/*  THE INTEGRITY HALF, and the one the old theory could
    not state.  In a trace where no screen was read, a payload accepted over
    the bearer is a payload that was sent: the session key needs both
    contributions, the contributions need the screens, and without the key
    the adversary cannot produce a term `Bearer_Take` will decrypt.

    THIS IS THE REMOTE ADVERSARY, and it is the whole of the gain.  Bluetooth
    reaches tens of metres and the service UUID is a constant in the
    repository, so before this ruling every carriage was readable and
    forgeable at that range.  Now the range is the range of reading a phone
    screen.  Nothing here weakens the Dolev-Yao adversary; the lemma simply
    asks what follows when it did not come close enough to look.

    MUTATION-TESTED 2026-10-05: restoring `Out` to BOTH contribution rules
    falsifies this in 8 steps.  **An earlier draft of this comment said one
    was enough. The mutation was run and one is not**, which is the whole
    reason a mutation claim gets run rather than reasoned.

    WHAT THE ONE-CONTRIBUTION MUTATION SHOWS, worth more than the claim it
    replaced.  Leak one contribution and both this lemma and the secrecy of
    the payload still VERIFY: the key is `h(<label, ca, cb>)` and an
    adversary holding one half derives nothing.  Only
    `a_contribution_is_secret_unless_a_screen_was_read` falsifies, as it
    must.  **The session therefore survives one readable screen and needs
    both**, which is why the key takes the two contributions rather than the
    ceremony-id, which a single screen would give away.                                          */
lemma a_payload_accepted_with_no_screen_read_was_sent:
  "All B A cid p #i.
       Accepted(B, A, cid, p) @ #i
     & not(Ex X Y kind v #o. Observed(X, Y, kind, v) @ #o)
     ==> (Ex #s. Sent(A, B, cid, p) @ #s)"

end
